IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `miles-card-domain`

§ `Terms and Conditions Governing the DBS Woman's Card Promotions and Programmes`

-- ===========================================================================
-- DOCUMENT. Source-bundle id `dbs-womans-card-tnc`; the document's own dating
-- line, on every page, is "Updated 27 Jul 2026". Six pages, clauses 1 to 23.
-- Private contract text, not law. Nothing here is advice about anyone's card.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ANSWERS. For ONE transaction on the DBS Woman's World
-- Card: how many DBS Points per S$5 it earns, what that is in miles per
-- dollar, whether the answer turns on a fact only the merchant's acquirer
-- holds, and what monthly cap applies. It answers from clauses 11 to 19.
--
-- NO RULE HERE COMPARES A STRING. Catala has no string type, and this corpus
-- must lower to Catala (spec section 2), so the string work is done once in
-- layer 1 (`classify.l4`) and every rule below reads a closed enumeration:
-- `Description pattern` for cl.11's Table 2, `Charge kind` for cl.11(iii) to
-- cl.11(vii), `posted in a foreign currency` for cl.12. The printed strings
-- are still on the `Transaction` and still on every citation, for a reader;
-- they are simply never inspected.
--
-- That is the SECOND PASS. The first encoded Table 2 as 90 `CONTAINS` calls
-- over the statement description, cl.11(v) to (vii) as description proxies,
-- and cl.11(iii) and (iv) as limbs that could not be decided at all. Two of
-- those three are strictly better now: the charge kinds decide (iii) to (vii)
-- on the fact the clause is about, rather than on a word DBS happens to print.
-- Table 2 is a lateral move with one loss recorded at its rule -- the mapping
-- from 90 printed terms to 19 families is a judgement, and the file can no
-- longer be checked against the printed table by string comparison alone.
--
-- WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT MODEL.
--
--   cl.20 to cl.23 -- the plain DBS WOMAN'S CARD (base 1X, bonus 4X on online
--     spend, same S$1,000 monthly cap). OUT OF SCOPE, because cl.15 confines
--     cl.16 to cl.19 to the Woman's WORLD Card and the two are different
--     products. The shared domain's Card enumeration carries no constructor
--     for the plain Woman's Card, so it cannot be asked for here either.
--     cl.15 is encoded as a predicate and asserted, but it is NOT applied as
--     a guard on the exported rule: see the note at that rule.
--   cl.3 (DBS may vary or terminate at will), cl.5 (DBS's decision is final)
--     and cl.7 (DBS may postpone awarding) -- discretions with no stated
--     trigger. They defeat any answer this module gives, which is a property
--     of the instrument and is recorded as a defeasibility site, not encoded.
--   cl.4 (personal data consent) -- not operative on what a transaction earns.
--   cl.8 (principal and supplementary spending consolidates) and cl.9 (points
--     on a reversed transaction are clawed back) -- operative on the ACCOUNT
--     over time, not on the rate this transaction earns. Out of scope with
--     the allocation problem (spec section 6).
--   cl.6 -- the DBS Rewards Programme Terms and Conditions, incorporated by
--     reference and NOT HELD in this corpus. They are the document that would
--     settle which transactions are "eligible" at all. Every answer carries
--     that as a condition string rather than as a silent assumption.
--
-- THE ONE DESIGN DECISION WORTH READING BEFORE THE RULES. Whether a
-- transaction is "online spend" is a fact about the MERCHANT, not about how
-- the cardholder paid. cl.13: "DBS determines an online transaction based on
-- system indicators. These indicators are decided by the merchant and their
-- banker, and passed to DBS when the transaction is posted to the Card." So a
-- mobile-wallet tap at a physical terminal earns the online bonus IF AND ONLY
-- IF the merchant's acquirer flags it online -- the wallet is irrelevant, and
-- so is the payment channel the cardholder saw. `credential` and `payment
-- channel` are read NOWHERE in this module, and that absence is the encoding
-- of cl.13. The illustrations at the end assert it in both directions.
--
-- cl.11's own words point the other way -- "made via the Internet" is a fact
-- about the cardholder's act -- and cl.13 is what DBS actually applies. That
-- tension is recorded as an interpretation fork, not silently resolved.
--
-- IGNORED PARAMETERS. The composing module passes (card, cardholder, month,
-- txn) to every card uniformly. This card reads ONLY `txn`.
-- It reads NEITHER `cardholder` NOR `month`: this T&C conditions nothing on a
-- standing cardholder fact, and the only monthly quantity it names is the
-- S$1,000 online-spend cap, which is reported as a cap rather than applied
-- (how much of a cap remains is the allocation problem, out of scope).
-- It no longer reads `card` either: cl.15 is encoded as a predicate and
-- asserted, but the guard that applied it used REFUSE, which is outside the
-- v1 Catala fragment. See the note at the exported rule.
--
-- RATES, NOT AMOUNTS. cl.10, cl.17(b), cl.18(b) and cl.19(d) all round the
-- awarded points DOWN to a whole number, and cl.19(d) rounds the online bonus
-- on the month's ACCUMULATED online spend, not per transaction. A `Rate
-- answer` carries a marginal rate and has nowhere to put either rounding, so
-- the figures here are exact rates before rounding, and every answer says so.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `11. Definitions and Exclusion, Online spend`

-- cl.11: "Online spend" refers to retail transaction for goods and services
-- made via the Internet and processed by the respective merchants/acquirers
-- as an online transaction through Mastercard Worldwide networks, excluding
-- the following transactions: (i) ... (viii).
--
-- The "through Mastercard Worldwide networks" conjunct is satisfied by the
-- card's identity: the DBS Woman's World Card is a World Mastercard, and the
-- shared domain gives it one constructor with no network axis (unlike DBS yuu,
-- which the domain splits Visa / American Express because its own T&C does).
-- So the network is not a fact this module tests; cl.15 carries it.

-- Table 1 of cl.11, all 57 codes, in the order the document prints them.
-- Read from the committed pdftotext extraction and emitted by a script, so
-- the ditto columns cannot drift; the description beside each code is the
-- document's own, for a reviewer checking a row against the printed table.
-- NOTE the document prints 6051 BEFORE 6050 -- that transposition is the
-- document's and is preserved here rather than tidied.
-- NOTE the first code is printed "0763"; L4 has one number for it, 763.
GIVEN mcc IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Table 1 of clause 11 lists the MCC` mcc MEANS
       mcc EQUALS  763   -- Agricultural Co-operatives
   OR  ^   ^      4784   -- Toll and Bridge Fees
   OR  ^   ^      4829   -- Money Transfer
   OR  ^   ^      4900   -- Utilities – Electric, Gas, Water, Sanitary
   OR  ^   ^      5047   -- Dental/Laboratory/Medical/Ophthalmic Hospital Equipment and Supplies
   OR  ^   ^      5199   -- Non-Durable Goods – Not Elsewhere Classified
   OR  ^   ^      5960   -- Direct Marketing – Insurance Services
   OR  ^   ^      5993   -- Cigar Stores and Stands
   OR  ^   ^      6010   -- Financial Institutions – Manual Cash Disbursements
   OR  ^   ^      6011   -- Financial Institutions – Automated Cash Disbursements
   OR  ^   ^      6012   -- Financial Institutions – Merchandise, Services and Debt Repayment
   OR  ^   ^      6051   -- Non-Financial Institutions – Foreign Currency, Liquid and Cryptocurrency Assets, Money Orders (not Money Transfer), Account Funding (not Stored Value Load), Travelers Cheques, and Debt Repayment
   OR  ^   ^      6050   -- Quasi Cash – Financial institutions, Merchandise, Services
   OR  ^   ^      6211   -- Security Brokers/Dealers
   OR  ^   ^      6300   -- Insurance Sales, Underwriting, and Premiums
   OR  ^   ^      6381   -- Insurance Premiums
   OR  ^   ^      6399   -- Insurance, Not Elsewhere Classified
   OR  ^   ^      6513   -- Real Estate Agents and Managers – Rentals
   OR  ^   ^      6529   -- Quasi Cash – Remote Stored Value Load – Financial Institute
   OR  ^   ^      6530   -- Quasi Cash – Remote Stored Value Load – Merchant
   OR  ^   ^      6534   -- Quasi Cash – Money Transfer – Member Financial Institution
   OR  ^   ^      6540   -- Non-Financial Institutions – Stored Value Card Purchase/Load
   OR  ^   ^      7261   -- Funeral Service and Crematories
   OR  ^   ^      7276   -- Tax Preparation Service
   OR  ^   ^      7311   -- Advertising Services
   OR  ^   ^      7322   -- Collection Agencies
   OR  ^   ^      7339   -- Stenographic and Secretarial Support
   OR  ^   ^      7349   -- Cleaning and Maintenance, Janitorial Services
   OR  ^   ^      7372   -- Computer Programming, Data Processing, and Integrated Systems Design Services
   OR  ^   ^      7375   -- Information Retrieval Services
   OR  ^   ^      7393   -- Detective Agencies, Protective Services and Security Services Including Armored Cars and Guard Dogs
   OR  ^   ^      7399   -- Business Services (Not Elsewhere Classified)
   OR  ^   ^      7511   -- Quasi Cash – Truck Stop Transactions
   OR  ^   ^      7523   -- Parking Lots, Parking Meters and Garages
   OR  ^   ^      7995   -- Betting including Lottery Tickets, Casino Gaming Chips, Off-track Betting, Wagers at Race Tracks and games of chance to win prizes of monetary value
   OR  ^   ^      8062   -- Hospitals
